---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Building ArcadeDuck | ArcadeDuck"
  description="Windows x64 build instructions for ArcadeDuck, including prerequisites, dependency packages, Visual Studio setup, and third-party source information."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
    <h1>Building ArcadeDuck from source</h1>
    <p class="page-description">
      ArcadeDuck currently supports building on Windows x64 using the
      Visual Studio/MSBuild workflow.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Requirements</p>
        <h2>What you need</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="planned-system-grid">
      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Required</span>
        <h3>Git</h3>
        <p>
          Used to clone ArcadeDuck and switch between the main development and
          experimental hardware branches.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Required</span>
        <h3>Visual Studio 2022</h3>
        <p>
          Install the <strong>Desktop development with C++</strong> workload
          and a Windows SDK.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Required</span>
        <h3>Windows x64 dependency package</h3>
        <p>
          ArcadeDuck uses a prebuilt third-party dependency bundle for the
          normal Windows build workflow.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Optional</span>
        <h3>LLVM / Clang</h3>
        <p>
          Install LLVM/Clang if you want to use one of the Clang-based build
          configurations.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Step 1</p>
        <h2>Clone ArcadeDuck</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/StillJC/ArcadeDuck.git
cd ArcadeDuck</code></pre>

    <p>
      For current development work, switch to the <code>dev</code> branch
      unless you specifically need one of the experimental hardware branches.
    </p>

    <pre><code>git switch dev</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Step 2</p>
        <h2>Install the Windows dependencies</h2>
      </div>

      <p>
        The dependency package is intentionally kept out of Git and distributed
        separately from the source repository.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      From the ArcadeDuck release assets, download the current files matching:
    </p>

    <pre><code>ArcadeDuck-Windows-x64-Dependencies-*.zip
ArcadeDuck-Windows-x64-Dependency-Sources-*.zip
SHA256SUMS.txt</code></pre>

    <p>
      The <strong>Dependencies</strong> ZIP is required to build ArcadeDuck.
      The <strong>Dependency-Sources</strong> ZIP preserves the corresponding
      third-party source archives and notices.
    </p>

    <p>
      Verify the dependency ZIP against <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code>, then
      extract the <strong>contents</strong> of the dependency ZIP directly into
      the ArcadeDuck repository root.
    </p>

    <p>After extraction, this file should exist:</p>

    <pre><code>dep\msvc\deps-x64\bin\uic.exe</code></pre>

    <p>Third-party package metadata is installed under:</p>

    <pre><code>dep\msvc\dependency-metadata\</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Step 3</p>
        <h2>Build the solution</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Open the Visual Studio solution:</p>

    <pre><code>ArcadeDuck.sln</code></pre>

    <p>
      Select an appropriate x64 configuration and build the solution in Visual
      Studio.
    </p>

    <p>
      The dependency package has been validated using a fresh ArcadeDuck
      worktree and the Visual Studio/MSBuild build path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Advanced</p>
        <h2>Rebuilding the dependency tree</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      The prebuilt dependency package is the recommended route for contributors.
      If you need to rebuild the Windows x64 third-party dependency tree
      yourself, ArcadeDuck includes:
    </p>

    <pre><code>scripts\deps\build-dependencies-windows-x64.bat</code></pre>

    <p>
      That script defines the dependency versions, source locations, and hashes
      used by the project. Rebuilding the full dependency set requires
      additional developer tools beyond a normal ArcadeDuck build.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Platform Development</p>
        <h2>Porting ArcadeDuck</h2>
      </div>

      <p>
        Windows x64 is the only currently supported and validated build target,
        but contributions for additional platforms and frontends are welcome.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="planned-system-grid">
      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Platform Port</span>
        <h3>Linux, Android, macOS, and other targets</h3>
        <p>
          New targets should use an appropriate native or reproducible
          dependency workflow rather than relying on the Windows dependency
          package.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Frontend Port</span>
        <h3>RetroArch / libretro</h3>
        <p>
          A libretro target is not just another operating-system build. It
          requires a frontend adapter for content loading, configuration,
          input, audio/video, arcade peripherals, external outputs, and other
          standalone ArcadeDuck behavior.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Architecture</span>
        <h3>Keep shared code portable</h3>
        <p>
          Platform-specific code should remain isolated from the shared emulator
          and arcade-hardware implementation wherever practical. New ports
          should avoid introducing Windows-only assumptions into shared code.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Contribution Workflow</span>
        <h3>Coordinate large ports early</h3>
        <p>
          Large platform or frontend ports should be discussed through GitHub
          before restructuring shared subsystems. Keep toolchain and dependency
          setup reproducible and document it with the target.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="systems-note">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Important</p>
      <h2>Visual Studio/MSBuild is the supported packaged workflow.</h2>
    </div>

    <p>
      Some generated Qt CMake metadata can contain paths from the machine on
      which Qt was originally built. The prebuilt dependency package should not
      currently be treated as a portable standalone CMake SDK.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="systems-note">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Third-Party Sources</p>
      <h2>Keep the source package with the dependency release.</h2>
    </div>

    <p>
      The dependency source package preserves the exact third-party source
      archives or commits corresponding to the published Windows x64 dependency
      bundle, together with source hashes and collected license/notice files.
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
